<hick:doc xmlns:hick="http://www.hickorydocs.com/1.0" weave="TEMPLATE.md">

# Cross-Platform Project Scaffolding

This template generates a multi-platform project with a consistent structure across:

- **SwiftUI** — iOS, iPadOS, and macOS using Apple's declarative UI framework
- **Jetpack Compose** — Android with Kotlin and modern declarative UI
- **Avalonia** — Cross-platform desktop (Windows, macOS, Linux) with .NET
- **React SPA** — Single-page web application with TypeScript
- **Browser Extension** — Chrome/Firefox extension with React TypeScript
- **Rust Backend** — API server using Axum for high performance

Each platform is behind a feature flag, so you only generate what you need.

## Usage

Generate a project with specific platforms:

```bash
hick --param project_name=MyApp --features swiftui,backend scaffolding/
```

Generate all platforms:

```bash
hick --param project_name=MyApp --features all scaffolding/
```

---

## Configuration Variables

These variables control project metadata and can be overridden via `--param`:

<hick:var name="project_name">MyProject</hick:var>
<hick:var name="bundle_id">com.example.myproject</hick:var>
<hick:var name="organization">Example Inc</hick:var>
<hick:var name="min_ios_version">17.0</hick:var>
<hick:var name="min_macos_version">14.0</hick:var>
<hick:var name="min_android_sdk">26</hick:var>
<hick:var name="target_android_sdk">34</hick:var>
<hick:var name="dotnet_version">8.0</hick:var>
<hick:var name="rust_edition">2024</hick:var>
<hick:var name="node_version">20</hick:var>

| Variable | Default | Purpose |
|----------|---------|---------|
| `project_name` | <hick:val name="project_name" /> | Project name (used in file paths, class names) |
| `bundle_id` | <hick:val name="bundle_id" /> | Reverse-domain bundle identifier |
| `organization` | <hick:val name="organization" /> | Company/organization name for copyright |
| `min_ios_version` | <hick:val name="min_ios_version" /> | Minimum iOS deployment target |
| `min_macos_version` | <hick:val name="min_macos_version" /> | Minimum macOS deployment target |
| `min_android_sdk` | <hick:val name="min_android_sdk" /> | Minimum Android SDK level |
| `target_android_sdk` | <hick:val name="target_android_sdk" /> | Target Android SDK level |
| `dotnet_version` | <hick:val name="dotnet_version" /> | .NET version for Avalonia |
| `rust_edition` | <hick:val name="rust_edition" /> | Rust edition year |
| `node_version` | <hick:val name="node_version" /> | Node.js version for web projects |

---

## Feature Definitions

Features control which platforms are generated. They can be combined freely.

<hick:feature name="swiftui" description="SwiftUI app for iOS, iPadOS, and macOS" />
<hick:feature name="compose" description="Jetpack Compose app for Android" />
<hick:feature name="avalonia" description="Avalonia app for Windows, macOS, and Linux" />
<hick:feature name="spa" description="React TypeScript single-page application" />
<hick:feature name="extension" description="Browser extension with React TypeScript" />
<hick:feature name="backend" description="Rust backend API server" />
<hick:feature name="rust-frontend-lib" description="Shared Rust library for frontends (WASM + FFI bindings)" />
<hick:feature name="sqlite" description="SQLite database support in Rust library" requires="rust-frontend-lib" />
<hick:feature name="iroh" description="Iroh peer-to-peer networking in Rust library" requires="rust-frontend-lib" />
<hick:feature name="iroh-relay" description="Iroh relay server for NAT traversal and peer discovery" />

**Build systems:**

<hick:feature name="just" description="Generate justfile for build automation (just command runner)" />
<hick:feature name="make" description="Generate Makefile for build automation (GNU make)" />

**Meta-features** for convenience:

<hick:feature name="all" description="Enable all platforms" requires="swiftui compose avalonia spa extension backend rust-frontend-lib" />
<hick:feature name="mobile" description="Mobile platforms" requires="swiftui compose" />
<hick:feature name="desktop" description="Desktop platforms" requires="swiftui avalonia" />
<hick:feature name="web" description="Web platforms" requires="spa extension" />
<hick:feature name="native" description="Native platforms with Rust library" requires="swiftui compose avalonia rust-frontend-lib" />

| Feature | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| `swiftui` | iOS, iPadOS, macOS with SwiftUI |
| `compose` | Android with Jetpack Compose |
| `avalonia` | Desktop with Avalonia (.NET) |
| `spa` | React TypeScript web app |
| `extension` | Browser extension |
| `backend` | Rust API server |
| `rust-frontend-lib` | Shared Rust library (WASM + FFI) |
| `sqlite` | SQLite database in Rust library (requires rust-frontend-lib) |
| `iroh` | Iroh P2P networking in Rust library (requires rust-frontend-lib) |
| `iroh-relay` | Iroh relay server for NAT traversal |
| `just` | Justfile for build automation |
| `make` | Makefile for build automation |
| `all` | All platforms (not build systems) |
| `mobile` | SwiftUI + Compose |
| `desktop` | SwiftUI + Avalonia |
| `web` | SPA + Extension |
| `native` | Native platforms + Rust library |

---

## Substitution Rules

The project name is substituted throughout all generated files using case variants:

<hick:substitute name="project_name" pattern="MyProject" value="{{project_name}}" variants="true" />

This means `MyProject`, `myProject`, `my_project`, `MY_PROJECT`, and `my-project`
in the template files will all be replaced with the corresponding case variant of
your chosen project name.

